--- body ---
## Context

Child of tracker 0557, runs when tier 1 (0560) finds no embedded outline.
No maintained, license-clean pure-JS heading classifier exists to vendor
(survey behind the ruling checked `pdf-struct`, Docling, `pdf.tocgen`,
GROBID -- Python/Java or copyleft, or both). The ruled answer is a
hand-rolled heuristic on `pdf.js`'s own positioned-text output
(`getTextContent()`: font size, weight, position per run).
**Deliberately not** seg/1's flat-text case-shape heuristic (Title Case /
ALL CAPS) -- the SOTA review found that close to non-signal on French
headings. Font size does not depend on a language's capitalization
convention, which is the whole reason this tier is expected to generalize
past English where seg/1's does not.

**Ruled out explicitly, same session**: no Python/Docling subprocess
bridge as an escalation path, even though Docling's MIT-licensed,
CPU-viable-without-OCR backend was the strongest non-JS candidate found.
Stays pure JS/TS on `pdf.js`'s own primitives, whatever quality ceiling
that implies.

**No third tier.** When this heuristic also comes up empty or
low-confidence, control falls through to seg/1 (0028) itself -- not a
separate PDF-page fixed-size fallback. seg/1 already owns confidence-gated
synthetic entries; duplicating that in PDF-page terms was explicitly
rejected (DECISIONS.md, same-day correction).

## Actions

1. Extract font size/weight/position per text run via `getTextContent()`.
2. Cluster by font-size jump (a heading-sized run is larger/bolder than
   the surrounding body text) and position (start-of-line, whitespace
   before/after) to produce heading candidates.
3. Score candidates into a confidence figure, the same shape seg/1 already
   uses (fraction of text inside confirmed entries).
4. Below whatever confidence bar is set (calibrate against real fixtures,
   don't guess a number), fall through to seg/1 -- do not build a
   fixed-size fallback here.

## Test

- A scanned/non-outline PDF with clear font-size heading structure --
  assert headings are found and roughly match a hand-checked answer.
- A PDF with no clear font-size structure (e.g. uniform body text
  throughout) -- assert the low-confidence path actually falls through to
  seg/1, and that the fallthrough is observable/testable, not silent.

## Exit criteria

- [ ] font-size/position clustering implemented
- [ ] confidence scoring matches seg/1's existing shape
- [ ] fallthrough to seg/1 on low confidence verified, not a third
      fallback tier
